  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and aloft will keep dry and ever\nwarmer conditions across the Quad State through Friday.\nTemperatures are expected to climb 10 or more degrees above\nnormal Friday through Saturday and possibly into Sunday.\n\nThe 00Z GFS has a nice swath of QPF spreading north through much\nof the Quad State through the day Saturday. This seems to be\nquite a stretch given the general lack of moisture return and\nupper support, and the NBM remains dry, as there is little \nsupport for it from any other model systems. Although rain is \nnot expected, a modest increase in cloud cover and humidity is \nlikely Saturday.\n\nThe weekend cold front has slowed down and now is not expected\nto pass until Sunday. That will keep the warm air around for\nmuch of the day over the southeast half of the region. As for\nprecipitation chances, the NBM PoPs are now primarily on Sunday\nand have increased to 30-50%, with the highest chances over the\nEvansville Tri State area. QPF is still expected to generally be\nunder a tenth of an inch. Instability is non-existent, so there\nis no thunder mention in this forecast. \n\nThe cold front will be rather healthy and will take temperatures\ndown below normal levels Monday through next Wednesday, as\nsprawling surface high pressure spreads over much of the country\neast of the Rockies.\n\nThe medium range guidance has not been able to focus a\nparticular time frame over the Thanksgiving weekend for a \ndecent chance of rain. However, there have been numerous \npossibilities put forth over the last few days, so it will not \nbe shock if some portion of the Quad State eventually see some \nrain.",
